{"yuzu_pack":1,"name":"Hyperkalemia in the ED","category":"Medicine::Emergency Medicine::Electrolytes","tags":["auto::System::Renal","auto::Specialty::Emergency Medicine"],"cards":[{"type":"cloze","text":"Earliest ECG change in hyperkalemia: {{c1::peaked (tented) T waves}}, best seen in {{c2::precordial leads}}.","extra":"Later: PR prolongation, QRS widening, sine wave.","image":"data:image/png;base64,"},{"type":"cloze","text":"Membrane stabilisation in hyperkalemia with ECG changes: {{c1::IV calcium gluconate 10% 10–30 mL}} (or calcium chloride via central line); onset {{c2::1–3 min}}, lasts {{c3::30–60 min}}.","extra":"Does not lower K+."},{"type":"basic","front":"Which treatments actually shift K+ into cells?","back":"Insulin 10 U IV with glucose, nebulised salbutamol 10–20 mg, sodium bicarbonate if acidotic."},{"type":"basic","front":"Which treatments remove K+ from the body?","back":"Loop diuretics, potassium binders (patiromer, SZC), haemodialysis."},{"type":"cloze","text":"Earliest ECG change in hyperkalemia: {{c1::peaked T waves}}."}]}